INTRO: question of perspective...
---------------------------------
Programming perspective (P-level)
>Versus<
C-Language perspective (L-level)
 
'P'rogramming perspective is a tiny but necessary "abstraction" built just above the 'L'anguage
("grass roots") perspective.
Our idea is to NOT "reason" directly with C language features, but with more "ad hoc" 
programming abstractions...

This 'P' layer follows very classical "procedural programming" orientation (*) on which we
elaborate actual programming patterns...

(*) "Structured programming" is obviously C's most natural programming orientation. Yet, the
language is quite versatile (some people would rather say "poor" or "lax" ...) to support other
paradigms (like "functional" paradigm)

This table shows main cases of correspondence between 'L' and 'P' concepts.
What must be understood is that the 'P' concepts derived from a given 'L' concept are usually
very different 'P' concepts. For example, from the point of view of 'P', 'functional macros' have
little to do with 'pure macros' - and yet both are just ordinary macros of the C preprocessor...
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| 'L': preprocessor / compiler                 | 'P': programming layer ("procedural          |
|                                              | programming" orientation)                    |
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| preprocessor macros                          | - constants & functional macros              |
|                                              | - pure macros                                |
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| c-values (basic types)                       | - values (integers & floating points)        |
|                                              | - statuses (int C type)                      |
|                                              | - sentinel values                            |
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| non void returning c-functions               | - (pure) functions                           |
|                                              | - procedures                                 |
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| void returning c-functions                   | technical routines (1)                       |
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| typedef                                      | used as simple alias definition for:         |
| (2)                                          | - custom function types                      |
|                                              | - handle types                               |
|                                              | - basic types aliasing (2)                   |
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| enum                                         | - statuses (unary, binary,...)               |
|                                              | - states representations                     |
|                                              | - standard values                            |
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| struct (union)                               | - private instances                          |
|                                              | - compound parameters                        |
|                                              | - description of data formats (3)            |
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| pointer (entity on which * operator is       | - address variables                          |
| applicable) (4)                              |   + single entity address (a_ indicator)     |
|                                              |   + array address: (s_ indicator)            |
|                                              | - generic (r_ indicator) parameter           |
|                                              | - handles                                    |
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| logical expressions                          | only boolean typeless VALUES exist - boolean |
|                                              | "type" MUST NOT be artificially "defined"    |
+----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
(1) Like 'P' procedures and functions, 'P' technical routines are code CONTAINERS. But
    contrary to procedures and functions, technical routines are NOT (directly) CALLABLE.
(2) Thus, from 'P'rogramming perspective, types abstractions are somewhat more subtle than
    rudimentary "typedef" constructions - that are actually simple names aliasing (*).
    By the way, we question the (in)famous "<type name>_t" types naming convention (like size_t,
    pthread_t, bool_t, etc.) which confines in fact the principle of data abstraction to a
    simple renomination of type.
    Extra comment: The motivation at that time was probably a vain attempt to "modernize" C
    framework vis-a-vis the new programming languages (those offering full-fledged type systems)
    of the epoch. Hence, the "initiative" was totally clumsy, and by the way largely
    counter-productive...
    (*) to emphasize our point of view, 'typedef' is a CARICATURE of type definition ; the point
    is just to understand and keep in mind the limitations (from 'P' perspective) of that
    'L'-level mechanism.
(3) that is representation of "shared" data structures (protocol messages, EDI, etc.).
    The method imposes STRICT control of data alignment, structure padding and packing in memory.
    The techniques "of course" exist (#pragma) in C, but generally have very bad impact on
    process performance, and, above all, are not portable - what is quite paradoxical when your
    purpose is to represent open specifications...
    Morality: from our point of view, this technique of data format representation is a "good"
    example of "false good idea"...  Finally, it's less complicated to rely on "grassroots"
    methods - those based on explicit offset and data length definitions and manipulations...
(4) That pointer definition given at L-level is disputable. Fortunately, our P-abstraction
    let us NOT having to deal with such philosophical (and somewhat vain) questions...


Programming main patterns and conventions:
------------------------------------------
- "Modular design" : THE (inalienable) principle...
  Modules (as designation of "code containers" that are source files) remain the FUNDAMENTAL
  conceptual "units" of software development. That "modular design", although "ancestral", is
   not obsolete with respect to more recent concepts, such as "class models" developed in
  so-called "object-oriented" programming... Modules remain the "essence" of preliminary
  software design (*).
  "Classes" may only make sense far later, during the "detailed" design process... (**)
  => Practical convention: one module <mod> exactly corresponds to:
  - single <mod>.h header file OR
  - <mod>.h header and <mod>.c source files (most common case) OR
  - single <mod>.c source file if and only if it implements main() function.
  (*) "Preliminary" does not just mean "draft" - immediatly forgotten when the "real"
      developement starts. "Preliminary" really means "fundamental"...
  (**) Actually, I feel that classes represent an incidental concept - or even an irrelevant
       one - in software design. It has to do with the confusion between analytical and
       synthetic thinking (judgement). TODO: cca a l'air passionnant... ag developper!!!... 

- C-Ansi / C-Posix split: the sources are splitted between "c-posix" and "c-ansi"
  sub-directories.
  Initially, the motivation was practical: such split eases recuperation of modules usable to
  write stand-alone C-Ansi programs (reminder: pure C-Ansi programs have limitations but offer
  the great advantage to run almost "anywhere")
  And even if now C-Ansi programs are less in our "focus", we realized that such a split
  "inducts" a good separation between pure business logic (code that can be written in Ansi-C)
  with "technical", low level code (that is "by essence" Posix-C). 
  NB: final extra, empiric, but real, advantage : this separation lets us directly identifying 
  our own headers (#include "c-ansi/xxx" and "c-posix/xxx")

- particular APIs:
  + "kitchen" paradigm: allows to maintain primal simplicity of "enriched" API.
    Analogy with some (contemporary) restaurants in which clients have an open view inside the
    kitchen...
    The public API is split in two levels :
    . the "lower" level is the "kitchen view" : define the complete API. Like the kitchen of the
      restaurant, the decoration and the beauty of the place does not really matter; but the
      neatness of that place must be irreproachable.
    . the "upper" level is like the "menu of the diner room" : is a simplification of the API,
      and is focused on most common usages.
    Example: conditional declarations (#if / #ifdef) quickly make public interfaces difficult to
    read and should be placed in the "ad hoc" kitchen interface.
  + "glue" paradigm: dedicated API meant to fix the approximations and inconsistencies of
    "standards" - that we MUST get along with...
    Notice: "universal glue" is an ANTI-PATTERN! (we are deeply engaged against "catchall" design
    syndrom)
  + "freeloader": a free-loader is a simple "technique" suitable for very light "singleton"
    modules. It consists to define the functions directly in the header (.h) file so that we
    formally get rid of the implementation (.c) file. Such modules are of course to be included
    in unique and specific place (the main module in general) of application.
    NB: example of freeloader can be found in "Flint" project...

 "Object oriented" programming in C (bah!):
 ------------------------------------------
- Instances and Handles (aka the "pure" objects)
  The foundational programming concept — the close association of code and data (methods and
  attributes) - can be easily implemented in C using instances and handles, driven by the philosophy that:
     "Should only exist object instances !!!"
  + Instance: This is simply an allocation of a data structure in memory (typically on the heap).
    Instances are defined strictly within the private section of a module to ensure encapsulation.
  + Handle: This is nothing more than a pointer variable that references the instance. The handle is the
    only public definition exposed to the outside world.A natural and essential property of handles is
    that multiple handles can be attached to a single instance (much like how a single piece of luggage
    can have multiple physical handles).
  Remarks: 
  + As usual, that purist vision - the tendency to view absolutely everything as an object - is a good
    basic principle. However, the assumption must sometimes be nuanced if we want to avoid nightmarish
    confusions and complications.
  + While a metaphor like hands and fingers might explain the connection between data and
    code more intuitively, let's not overcomplicate things. To keep it simple and avoid further
    confusion, we will stick to the standard terms: 'objects' and 'methods'."
  Some object derivations:
  + Object virtualization. This principle replaces the METHOD virtualization of "classes-based"
    programming languages. Advantages:
    . easily implementable in simple C
    . NO compilation/execution overhead
    . does NOT undermine the encapsulation principle
    . more accurate "objects" modelling (NO NEED for hazy "inheritance" principle(s)...)
    (Drawbacks: NONE)
  + Unique instance: also known as "singleton" principle... Emphases that even "global"
    instances (that are unique per process) MUST be formalized as regular instances.
    (that is referenced via explicit handle)
    This ensures respect of clear (instanciation and) initialization / execution / destruction
    cycle.
  + Pure constructors and destructors. Constructors and destructors are two "special" methods of
    our objects; their role is purely "technical" and must be clearly distinguished from
    other "functional" methods... Therefore, from a functional point of view, such methods may
    not fail. In practice, this means that the C functions which implement constructors and
    destructors always return the unary status "RETURNED"; any "technical" problem would give
    rise of an ANOMALY...
  + Factories: a "factory" instance is meant to create "product" instances.
    In most cases, we do not distinguish "factory" with "product" instances as objects; the
    "product" instance constructor remains a method of the "product" object. With the factory
    paradigm, the "product" constructor just becomes a "functional" method of the "factory"
    object. The factory paradigm is usefull to make easier arbitrary objects instance creation
    by other objects. That is, the factory mainly provides an "abstraction" of the paramaters
    used for the creation of product instances...
    Our strong recommandation is to resort to the factory paradigm only when necessary...

- Green items and collections:
  The green item is an intermediate concept of compound data between the bare struct-based data
  (see subjectication and proto-object concepts below) and the encapsulated data (in "pure object
  instance"). Green item concept is thus the first amendment of "everything is pure object" holistic
  vision...
  See also c-ansi/green.topo
  
- Subjectification: Reducing Everything to "me"
  We have already discussed the two most structured forms of data–function association: 
  the pure object, whose representation is hidden behind an opaque handle and can be accessed only through
  its methods; and the green item, whose structure is publicly defined but is nevertheless manipulated
  through a coherent family of functions.
  What about the remaining data structures - ordinary structures that do not belong to either category? At
  first sight, they may appear to be merely anonymous collections of data, passed as parameters to unrelated
  functions.
  However, these structures can still be given a clearer conceptual role. Whenever a function primarily
  operates on one of its parameters, that parameter may be designated as the function's subject.
  For example, instead of writing:
    int DoSomethingWithTitiAndTutu(struct TITI *titi, struct TUTU *tutu,/* other parameters */);
  we may designate titi as the central parameter and write:
    int TitiDoSomethingWithTutu(struct TITI *me,struct TUTU *tutu,/* other parameters */);
  The technical difference is minimal: the subject is placed first in the parameter list, following the
  convention already used for object handles and green-item pointers. The more important difference is
  conceptual. The function is presented as an operation performed by or on TITI, rather than as a completely
  general operation involving two unrelated structures.
  This practice may be called subjectification: treating an otherwise ordinary data structure as the explicit
  subject of the operations that primarily concern it.
  Subjectification does not make the structure an object. It does not imply data encapsulation, private state,
  dynamic dispatch, or any other specifically object-oriented property. It is simply a naming and organization
  convention that gives the data structure a recognizable relationship with the functions that operate on it.
  Thus, the general pattern becomes:
    ReturnType TypeDoSomething(struct TYPE *me,/* other parameters */);
  The name 'me' emphasizes that the first parameter is the principal participant in the operation. It provides
  a lightweight, object-like organization for ordinary C data without claiming that the data structure is a
  formal object or a green item.
  In this sense, subjectification extends the "everything revolves around me" principle to structures that
  do not qualify for the more distinguished status of object or green item.

- Proto-objects: a way to decompose a complex object into internally
  structured, object-like responsibilities within the same module.
  Consider a complex object, CO, implemented by the corresponding CO module.
  During its design, we may identify a simpler component, SO, responsible for
  a distinct sub-part of CO's behaviour or state.
  If SO has a sufficiently general purpose and could reasonably be reused by
  other objects or modules, the appropriate solution is straightforward:
  extract it from the CO module and implement it as a proper, independent SO
  object in its own module.
  The more interesting case is when SO is meaningful only as part of CO. Its
  responsibilities are real and distinct, but it has no useful existence
  outside CO. In that case, keeping SO inside the CO module is usually the
  right design. SO may then be treated as a proto-object.
  A proto-object is an internal, object-like component: it has its own data
  structure and its own set of operations, but it is not intended to provide
  an independently usable public abstraction.

  Two implementation styles are possible:
  + Strict internal-object style
    SO may be designed exactly as a proper object, with an opaque instance
    representation and a family of SO operations. This can still improve the
    local organization of the module.
    However, encapsulation is only partial in practice. Since CO and SO are
    implemented in the same module, the CO implementation necessarily has
    access to SO's private representation. The module boundary protects SO
    from external users, but it cannot protect SO from its containing object.
  + Lightweight, subjectified style
    Alternatively, SO may be represented by an ordinary visible structure,
    with operations conventionally organized around it. For example:
        int SoInitialize(struct SO *me, ...);
        int SoDoSomething(struct SO *me, ...);
    In this form, SO is not a pure object: its data need not be hidden behind
    a handle, and its operations may even be implemented as macros or inline
    helpers rather than ordinary functions.
    Nevertheless, SO remains formally separated from CO. Its state belongs
    to the SO structure, and the code manipulating that state is expressed as
    operations whose principal subject is SO - conventionally the first
    parameter, named 'me'.

  Proto-objects can therefore be understood as subjectified internal data
  structures. They do not necessarily provide strict encapsulation, nor do
  they claim the status of independent objects. Their purpose is instead to
  make internal responsibilities explicit: CO remains the public object,
  while SO is a clearly identified internal subject with its own data and
  operations.

  The benefit is primarily architectural clarity. Even where strict data
  hiding is neither possible nor useful, separating CO and SO responsibilities
  prevents the implementation of a complex object from becoming a single
  undifferentiated body of data and procedures.

- Recap:
  +--------------------------+--------------------------------------+------------------------+
  | Paradigm / Compound Data | Architectural Purpose                | Instantiation Location |
  +--------------------------+--------------------------------------+------------------------+
  | Pure Objects             | Encapsulated data structures;        | Heap (best technical   |
  |                          | strict emulation of OOP objects.     | choice)                |
  +--------------------------+--------------------------------------+------------------------+
  | Green Items              | Direct association of attributes and | Heap (by principle of  |
  |                          | methods; no encapsulation.           | green items)           |
  +--------------------------+--------------------------------------+------------------------+
  | Subjects                 | Formal association of data and       | Heap / Stack           |
  |                          | functions only.                      |                        |
  +--------------------------+--------------------------------------+------------------------+
  | Proto-Object             |                 Inherited  (Matches Parent)                   |
  +--------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+
  
