- The Dual Nature of Data: Logical Types vs. Physical Formats
  The traditional, monolithic concept of a "data type" is an incomplete abstraction. To accurately
  reason about data across complex systems, we must decouple its logical value (the abstract
  meaning within the program's domain) from its physical composition (its concrete materialization
  in memory or storage).
  + The Logical Value Type: Defines what the data represents and the valid operations allowed on
    it by human logic (e.g., a Date, a Temperature, or an Email Address).
  + The Physical Composition Type: Dictates how the data is encoded in hardware or disk storage
    (e.g., an ASCII string, a 64-bit float, or a Binary integer).
  Why the Illusion Works in Everyday Programming
  In standard, day-to-day programming scenarios, conflating these two aspects into a single
  monolithic "data type" is not an issue at all. In fact, it is highly efficient. When you
  declare a simple variable like int counter or float price, the logical value maps so cleanly to
  the physical memory structure that the distinction is entirely invisible.
  Because this abstraction works perfectly in these banal situations, it is completely normal that
  many developers view the separation between logical and physical types as an insignificant,
  purely academic subtlety.
  In a self-contained local scope, the physical composition is the logical value.

  Where the Monolithic Concept Breaks Down
  However, in the general case—most notably when working with databases, distributed systems, or
  cross - platform APIs - treating these two layers as a single entity completely falls apart.
  When we cleanly isolate the logical from the physical, data operations become vastly more flexible.
  Two data points d1 and d2 are perfectly and simply comparable provided that their logical
  value is of the same type (e.g., both represent the calendar date September 17, 2026),
  regardless of their physical composition types, which may be totally different (e.g., d1
  is stored as a raw ISO-8601 ASCII string, while d2 is a Binary Epoch timestamp).

  The Critique of Modern Database Systems
  It is therefore highly regrettable that mainstream database management languages, particularly
  SQL, completely overlook this dual nature. By forcing developers to define database columns
  strictly by their physical storage structures (like VARCHAR or INT) rather than true logical
  domains, SQL conflates storage with meaning.
  Because it lacks native, first-class support for logical types independent of physical layouts,
  it forces the application layer to manually handle domain validations, denying developers true
  data independence.



- General Principles for Practical Code Quality

   + The Golden Benchmark: The Empty Program Model
     A simple psychological brake to stop you from writing over-engineered junk.The only flawless,
     perfectly secure, and entirely bug-free software in existence is the empty program - a file
     containing exactly zero lines of code. It is universally compatible, runs instantly, and
     requires zero maintenance. Unfortunately, its commercial utility is non-existent. To make
     software actually do something, we common programmers are forced to compromise this perfection
     by introducing our own messy, error-prone code. Arrogant developers view coding as an
     act of creation; pragmatic developers recognize it as an act of contamination. Every
     single line of code you add is a liability, a future bug, and a maintenance burden.
     Your goal is not to see how much code you can produce, but to see how close you can leave
     your solution to the pristine efficiency of the empty program.


- General tips for achieving a roughly acceptable programming quality:
  + Empty program model: A simple "trick" to keep in mind to refrain from writing too much
    "crap"...
    The pure and perfect program exists. It is still unique but universal. This is of course the
    "empty" program - without a single line of code. Obviously its practical usefulness is very
    limited... To give it a use, we common programmers know no other way than to spoil this
    perfect program with our rotten lines of code...
  + Have good usage of binary statutes in C-programs (see also c-verities.topo, section "Why do
    guys so often harass girls ???") to avoid the following undesirable behaviours :
    . Hanging programms (with resource grabbing as major "side effect") may be simple consequence
      of a bad re-interpration of 'yes'/'no way' (girl verity perception) binary status as a
      'success'/'try again' (guy verity perception). 
    . Unexpected crashes may a contrario results of a wrong re-interpreation of 'success'/'try
      again' binary status as 'yes'/'no way' (shy people verity perception).
      Note: in many applications, there is a often mix of the two confusions....
    . Inefficient programs (senile "assitants", etc.)  may be caused by abusive re-interpretation
      of 'ok'/'ok, but' binary status into unary 'ok' status (like the European Union, with his
      habit of proudly presenting a total FAILURE to reach agreement as a SUCCESS of plenary
      agreement - "We unanimously all agreed... that we agree on nothing...")
   + More generally, avoid the pitfall of excessive dualisation of the truth. In particular, take 
     the time to consider the unary and ternary verities, which are most relevant in many 
     situations. (see also c-verities.topo, section 'Verities in computing')

